WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.125 --> 00:00:02.500
Narrator: The National Library
of Medicine proudly presents:

00:00:02.500 --> 00:00:04.167
Farmtastic Plastic.

00:00:04.167 --> 00:00:05.750
Starring Polly Mer.

00:00:05.750 --> 00:00:09.000
Join her on the farm
to help build plastics.

00:00:12.375 --> 00:00:15.333
Olivia: Time for
Environmental Games!

00:00:15.333 --> 00:00:17.208
I love these free games!

00:00:21.583 --> 00:00:24.333
Polly Mer: Join me and help
earthlings make good choices

00:00:24.333 --> 00:00:25.833
about plastics.

00:00:28.042 --> 00:00:30.667
Welcome to Farmtastic Plastic.

00:00:30.667 --> 00:00:33.708
Our mission is to collect the
goods to make plastics here on

00:00:33.708 --> 00:00:34.833
the Plastic Farm.

00:00:37.375 --> 00:00:40.458
Get Pickin'.

00:00:40.458 --> 00:00:42.750
Olivia: Um, these
aren't tomatoes?

00:00:42.750 --> 00:00:44.708
Polly Mer: This
is a plastic farm!

00:00:44.708 --> 00:00:48.625
Those are monomers, or single
chemical molecules used to build

00:00:48.625 --> 00:00:51.083
long molecules called polymers.

00:00:51.083 --> 00:00:53.792
Olivia: So all of these
are different monomers!

00:00:53.792 --> 00:00:55.833
There are so many types!

00:00:55.833 --> 00:00:58.833
Polly Mer: Monomers are the
building blocks of plastics.

00:00:58.833 --> 00:01:01.625
They are used to make
different types of plastics.

00:01:01.625 --> 00:01:02.833
Let’s collect some.

00:01:02.833 --> 00:01:04.208
Don’t forget the gold!

00:01:09.042 --> 00:01:10.417
Yeehaw!

00:01:10.417 --> 00:01:13.667
We have collected enough
types to earn a star!

00:01:13.667 --> 00:01:15.167
Narrator: Expand your Farm!

00:01:15.167 --> 00:01:20.708
Collect 3 styrene, 3 isocyanate,
and 3 polyethylglycol

00:01:22.417 --> 00:01:24.333
Olivia: So much
for free gaming.

00:01:24.333 --> 00:01:26.375
Polly Mer: Let's check
out that patch over there.

00:01:30.083 --> 00:01:31.500
Olivia: These are big ones!

00:01:31.500 --> 00:01:32.583
Polly Mer: Yes.

00:01:32.583 --> 00:01:35.833
Monomers can be simple or
complex - these monomers are

00:01:35.833 --> 00:01:38.208
larger molecules
with more atoms.

00:01:38.208 --> 00:01:42.458
Olivia: Ethylene
oxide and Bisphenol A!

00:01:42.458 --> 00:01:45.875
Styrene and
Dimethylteraphthalate!

00:01:45.875 --> 00:01:47.500
Narrator: Want to build faster?

00:01:47.500 --> 00:01:49.958
Get more monomer baskets!

00:01:49.958 --> 00:01:52.208
Polly Mer: Sounds like
a pretty good deal to me!

00:01:52.208 --> 00:01:53.542
Olivia: Pushy aren’t they?

00:01:53.542 --> 00:01:55.375
Looks like we’ve filled
our basket again.

00:01:55.375 --> 00:01:56.833
Narrrator: Congratulations!

00:01:56.833 --> 00:02:00.500
You've unlocked the
Polymerization Mill!

00:02:00.500 --> 00:02:01.667
Olivia: Polymers?

00:02:01.667 --> 00:02:04.542
Polly Mer: Polymers are just
long chains of monomers that are

00:02:04.542 --> 00:02:07.333
chemically connected
through covalent bonds.

00:02:07.333 --> 00:02:10.667
Covelant bonds are the strongest
bonds between atoms.

00:02:10.667 --> 00:02:12.958
Olivia: Um, they
won't connect!

00:02:12.958 --> 00:02:13.833
Polly Mer: That’s right!

00:02:13.833 --> 00:02:17.333
Remember – plastics are
SYNTHETIC polymers,

00:02:17.333 --> 00:02:20.167
made of monomers combined
through a chemical reaction,–

00:02:20.167 --> 00:02:23.625
not naturally occurring in
nature – but made by humans.

00:02:23.625 --> 00:02:27.333
Humans discovered how
to make plastics in 1856.

00:02:27.333 --> 00:02:30.875
Olivia: Okay- but how do we
get them to stick together?

00:02:30.875 --> 00:02:32.125
Polly Mer: It depends!

00:02:32.125 --> 00:02:35.250
Polymerization reactions
need heat, pressure, or

00:02:35.250 --> 00:02:38.542
the presence of a catalyst,
another chemical, in order to

00:02:38.542 --> 00:02:41.500
connect the monomers
together to form polymers.

00:02:41.500 --> 00:02:44.125
Olivia: So that’s why we
need the Polymerization

00:02:44.125 --> 00:02:47.208
Reaction Mill– it provides
the things needed to

00:02:47.208 --> 00:02:49.667
combine the monomers
into polymers!

00:02:49.667 --> 00:02:50.792
Polly Mer: Let’s try it.

00:02:50.792 --> 00:02:53.792
This one is using the catalyst
Titanium (III) chloride to

00:02:53.792 --> 00:02:57.125
speed up the reaction –
grab some ethene monomers!

00:03:01.250 --> 00:03:05.375
Olivia: Wow- ethene monomers
are expensive to produce!

00:03:05.375 --> 00:03:06.833
Narrator: Polymerizing

00:03:06.833 --> 00:03:09.833
Olivia: Ugh! We have to wait.

00:03:09.833 --> 00:03:12.292
Polly Mer: Time to browse
the Monomer pellet catalog!

00:03:12.292 --> 00:03:15.667
Limited edition monomers -
Only $1.99.

00:03:15.667 --> 00:03:18.375
Olivia: I thought my Biology
teacher told me we have polymers

00:03:18.375 --> 00:03:22.500
in our bodies – we don’t have
plastic in us though, do we?

00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:24.500
Polly Mer: (laughing)
No Plastic.

00:03:24.500 --> 00:03:28.500
We do have NATURAL polymers–
occurring in nature without

00:03:28.500 --> 00:03:32.792
human intervention- things
like DNA, Protein, and

00:03:32.792 --> 00:03:35.500
polysaccharides,
a type of sugar.

00:03:35.500 --> 00:03:38.125
These are all natural
polymers made of various

00:03:38.125 --> 00:03:39.708
natural monomers.

00:03:39.708 --> 00:03:43.042
They require cellular energy
and enzymes to catalyze those

00:03:43.042 --> 00:03:45.167
polymerization reactions.

00:03:45.167 --> 00:03:46.833
Narrator: 1 hour sale!

00:03:46.833 --> 00:03:51.333
Olivia: Yeah, nothing predatory
going on here at all...

00:03:51.333 --> 00:03:53.083
Look! It's done!

00:03:55.708 --> 00:03:59.667
Olivia: Oh look- they are
all stuck together now-

00:03:59.667 --> 00:04:02.417
I can’t pull them apart!

00:04:02.417 --> 00:04:06.625
Oh I see- the double bonds
from the monomers are now

00:04:06.625 --> 00:04:08.292
just single bonds!

00:04:08.292 --> 00:04:11.458
That’s how the monomers
were able to stick together:

00:04:11.458 --> 00:04:14.333
the double bond in the
monomer is broken and

00:04:14.333 --> 00:04:18.292
a single chemical bond is
formed between the monomers.

00:04:18.292 --> 00:04:21.542
Polly Mer: Yep- a
covalent bond- very strong.

00:04:21.542 --> 00:04:25.333
This is polyethene sometimes
called polyethylene.

00:04:25.333 --> 00:04:27.833
Even though it’s a polymer
now, it’s named after

00:04:27.833 --> 00:04:30.083
the monomer molecule ethene.

00:04:30.083 --> 00:04:32.583
Olivia: But what if I used
a different monomer?

00:04:32.583 --> 00:04:34.250
Polly Mer: You’d make
a different polymer.

00:04:34.250 --> 00:04:37.833
Polymers can be made of only one
type of monomer – or different

00:04:37.833 --> 00:04:39.958
repeating types of monomers.

00:04:39.958 --> 00:04:43.042
Olivia: Wait a second –
this is just like a string-

00:04:43.042 --> 00:04:45.833
how is this plastic??

00:04:45.833 --> 00:04:48.167
How does this hold soda pop?

00:04:48.167 --> 00:04:53.000
Narrator: Come play in
our other game world!

00:04:53.000 --> 00:04:56.375
Poly Mer: Plastics, as most
people think of them—soda bottles,

00:04:56.375 --> 00:05:00.292
furniture, phone cases—are just
lots of those little strands

00:05:00.292 --> 00:05:01.708
next to each other.

00:05:01.708 --> 00:05:04.542
How they are held together
though defines the two

00:05:04.542 --> 00:05:06.875
Thermoplastics and Thermosets.

00:05:06.875 --> 00:05:11.083
Thermoplastics are sheets of
polymer strands held together

00:05:11.083 --> 00:05:13.583
through weak
intermolecular bonds.

00:05:13.583 --> 00:05:16.750
When heated – the strands
separate – the plastic melts,

00:05:16.750 --> 00:05:19.167
and can be remolded
into different shapes.

00:05:19.167 --> 00:05:21.708
These types of plastics
can be truly recycled

00:05:21.708 --> 00:05:23.000
into something else.

00:05:23.000 --> 00:05:26.417
Thermoset plastics – are held
together through strong covalent

00:05:26.417 --> 00:05:29.708
chemical bonds between the
molecules of one strand and

00:05:29.708 --> 00:05:33.417
another-created through a
process called cross-linking--

00:05:33.417 --> 00:05:37.083
this makes a very strong matrix
that won’t melt when heated.

00:05:37.083 --> 00:05:39.750
It also means that you
can’t make a new item

00:05:39.750 --> 00:05:41.333
out of those plastics.

00:05:41.333 --> 00:05:43.458
You can repurpose the
plastic by chopping it into

00:05:43.458 --> 00:05:45.833
small pieces- but
it will never melt.

00:05:45.833 --> 00:05:48.875
Olivia: Okay- so the
thermoplastics are like

00:05:48.875 --> 00:05:53.625
ice cubes- I can melt them and
refreeze them into different

00:05:53.625 --> 00:05:57.833
shapes whereas the thermosets
are like a raw egg –

00:05:57.833 --> 00:06:01.250
once I cook it- it can
never be a raw egg again

00:06:01.250 --> 00:06:02.500
Polly Mer: Exactly!

00:06:02.500 --> 00:06:04.417
Narrator: Make
the thermoplastic:

00:06:04.417 --> 00:06:07.667
Polyvinylchloride, PVC!

00:06:07.667 --> 00:06:08.750
Olivia: Here we go!

00:06:12.333 --> 00:06:14.667
Narrator: Make your PVC Blue!

00:06:14.667 --> 00:06:16.333
Olivia: I hadn’t
thought about that-

00:06:16.333 --> 00:06:18.375
plastics do come in all colors.

00:06:22.792 --> 00:06:25.167
Narrator: Make your
plastic more light weight!

00:06:25.167 --> 00:06:26.958
Add some filler!

00:06:26.958 --> 00:06:30.333
Polly Mer: Fillers make the
plastic lighter, reduce costs,

00:06:30.333 --> 00:06:33.958
stabilize, and sometimes
make them somewhat fire proof.

00:06:39.917 --> 00:06:42.000
Narrator: Your PVC
is too brittle!

00:06:42.000 --> 00:06:43.417
Plasticize it!

00:06:43.417 --> 00:06:46.875
Olivia: I thought we
just made plastic!

00:06:47.708 --> 00:06:49.833
Polly Mer: Plasticizers are
chemicals added to the mix of

00:06:49.833 --> 00:06:53.000
monomers to give the
plastic different properties.

00:06:53.000 --> 00:06:55.792
Phthalates are chemical
plasticizers that are used in

00:06:55.792 --> 00:06:58.375
PVC to make them more flexible.

00:06:58.375 --> 00:06:59.542
Olivia: Finally!

00:07:01.667 --> 00:07:03.167
Weird!

00:07:03.167 --> 00:07:05.167
It smells funny!

00:07:05.167 --> 00:07:07.000
Polly Mer: Those are the
plasticizers escaping the

00:07:07.000 --> 00:07:10.042
plastic – they can
be health hazards.

00:07:10.042 --> 00:07:13.583
Narrator: Make the
thermoset plastic: Bakelite

00:07:16.167 --> 00:07:17.333
Olivia: Weird!

00:07:17.333 --> 00:07:21.792
This time our
ingredients are liquid!

00:07:21.792 --> 00:07:25.167
Polly Mer: Yes, remember these
types form covalent bonds

00:07:25.167 --> 00:07:27.917
between polymer strands
after they cure or set.

00:07:27.917 --> 00:07:31.708
They are usually in liquid form
to begin with and over a period

00:07:31.708 --> 00:07:34.375
of time will become
a hard solid plastic.

00:07:34.375 --> 00:07:36.875
Many of these are
used as coatings- like

00:07:36.875 --> 00:07:38.500
polyurethane on furniture.

00:07:38.500 --> 00:07:39.792
Narrator: Polymerizing.

00:07:39.792 --> 00:07:43.333
Time remaining: 24 hours.

00:07:43.333 --> 00:07:45.000
Olivia: 24 hours!!!!

00:07:45.000 --> 00:07:47.208
Guess I’ll check
on that tomorrow.

00:07:47.208 --> 00:07:48.750
Polly Mer: Time to
feed the fungus!

00:07:48.750 --> 00:07:50.125
Olivia: Pardon me?

00:07:50.125 --> 00:07:52.333
Polly Mer: This may be
a Plastic Farm – but

00:07:52.333 --> 00:07:54.500
we still have critters to feed!

00:07:54.500 --> 00:07:57.875
This fungus is one of the few
species of fungi and bacteria

00:07:57.875 --> 00:08:01.292
found to digest synthetic
polymers like plastics.

00:08:01.292 --> 00:08:05.333
But they only decompose specific
plastics in specific conditions–

00:08:05.333 --> 00:08:08.167
so they are not enough to
save earth from plastics

00:08:08.167 --> 00:08:09.458
that are not recycled.

00:08:09.458 --> 00:08:11.333
That one wants PVC.

00:08:11.333 --> 00:08:16.667
Olivia: So humans CAN choose
to produce some plastics that

00:08:16.667 --> 00:08:18.083
will be better for the planet!

00:08:18.083 --> 00:08:19.667
Narrator: 6 hours later

00:08:19.667 --> 00:08:21.708
Olivia's Dad: Olivia? Honey?

00:08:21.708 --> 00:08:25.917
What are Farm bills, and why
did I just get a call from the

00:08:25.917 --> 00:08:28.542
phone company about a
$500 charge for them?

